</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Disseminated coccidioidomycosis typically presents with lesions involving the skin, bones, and meninges. Laboratory findings often include eosinophilia and leukocytosis. Additionally, sporangia may be observed in pus, sputum, or cerebrospinal fluid, and radiographic studies may show nodular pulmonary infiltrates with thin-walled cavities and hilar adenopathy. Serologic testing is helpful for diagnosis and prognosis, with persistent high-titer IgG indicating disseminated disease.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

