<GROUND_TRUTH>
The fragmentation patterns and resulting fragment ions for the given compounds are as follows:

(a) **2,2,3,3-Tetramethylbutane (C₈H₁₈)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 114) fragments to form a tertiary carbocation (most stable), resulting in a base peak at m/z 57.  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_8\text{H}_{18}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_4\text{H}_9^+ \, (\text{m/z 57}) + \cdot \text{C}_4\text{H}_9 \, (\text{radical, not detected})
  \]

(b) **2-Methylpentane (C₆H₁₄)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 86) fragments to lose a methyl radical (·CH₃, mass 15), yielding a peak at m/z 71.  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_6\text{H}_{14}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_5\text{H}_{11}^+ \, (\text{m/z 71}) + \cdot \text{CH}_3
  \]

(c) **1-Butanol (C₄H₁₀O)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 74) can lose water (H₂O, mass 18), yielding a peak at m/z 56.  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_4\text{H}_{10}\text{O}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_4\text{H}_8^+ \, (\text{m/z 56}) + \text{H}_2\text{O}
  \]

(d) **Ethylbenzene (C₈H₁₀)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 106) undergoes alpha-cleavage to lose an ethyl radical (·C₂H₅, mass 29), yielding a peak at m/z 77 (tropylium ion).  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_8\text{H}_{10}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_7\text{H}_7^+ \, (\text{m/z 77}) + \cdot \text{C}_2\text{H}_5
  \]

(e) **1-Bromobutane (C₄H₉Br)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 136) loses a bromine radical (·Br, mass 79/81), yielding a peak at m/z 57 (butyl cation).  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_4\text{H}_9\text{Br}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_4\text{H}_9^+ \, (\text{m/z 57}) + \cdot \text{Br}
  \]

(f) **N,N-Dimethylethylamine (C₄H₁₁N)**  
- The molecular ion (m/z 73) undergoes alpha-cleavage to lose a methyl radical (·CH₃, mass 15), yielding a peak at m/z 58.  
- Fragmentation:  
  \[
  \text{C}_4\text{H}_{11}\text{N}^+ \rightarrow \text{C}_3\text{H}_8\text{N}^+ \, (\text{m/z 58}) + \cdot \text{CH}_3
  \]

These fragmentation patterns are typical for each compound, with the most stable carbocations or resonance-stabilized ions dominating the spectra.
